Hmmm... A more down-to-earth example of the same general case is the $^&*@
developer who builds a high-rise condo (or billboard, or drive-in movie
theater) between you and the lovely view which caused you to buy your
house.  Somebody is preventing you from receiving photons you want (or
bombarding you with photons you don't want).

I don't remember how this type of thing was handled in _The Machinery of
Friedman_ ;-).  My guess is that 1) enough customers of enough PPL systems
(remember, we're talking about multiple competing systems of law, not a
monopoly) would want to see the sky that they would exert market pressure
on the systems to which they subscribe to make such obstruction of the sky
a tort deserving of compensation, and those systems would in turn exert
pressure on non-conforming systems to get in line or provide compensation. 
Or, 2) very few people would care, and such billboards would become
commonplace.  Star lovers would be screwed (or, more sensibly, get off the
Earth).

Utopia is not an option.  I don't expect a PPL to be 100% in line with my
personal preferences (I'd like the privilege of shooting at people who go
35 in the left-hand lane on the freeway, for example, but I'm unlikely to
get it :-), but I think it'll be a damn sight better than democracy.

>The second relates to access to businesses for the handicapped.  Currently,
>many businesses are required by law to make themselves accessible to the
>handicapped in various ways, e.g. put in ramps in addition to stairs,
>provide braille menus, etc.  There can't really be sufficient economic
