Date: Tue, 24 Aug 93 16:11:07 EDT
From: Brian.Hawthorne@east.sun.com (Brian Holt Hawthorne - SunSelect Engineering)
Subject: PPL Questions

> The first is the outfit from Georgia that's been proposing putting up
> mile-long billboards into low Earth orbit.  In addition to being unsightly
> and annoying, they would seriously screw astronomical observation.  What
> could be done (if anything) about this situation in a PPL?

The same thing that could be done in a PPL if your neighbor decides
to build a 6-story cinder-block box that blocks your heretofor 
wonderful view of the river.

> The second relates to access to businesses for the handicapped.  Currently,
> many businesses are required by law to make themselves accessible to the
> handicapped in various ways, e.g. put in ramps in addition to stairs,
> provide braille menus, etc.  There can't really be sufficient economic
> incentive to do these sort of things: how many blind customers does the
> average restaurant get?  Would the handicapped simply be SOL in a PPL?

Actually, many businesses find that there ARE real paybacks for these 
changes. For example, "handicapped" ramps really ought to be called
"baby carriage" ramps. Once a business installs ramps, they suddenly
find that they get scads more custom from those of us with offspring in
wheeled vehicles.

In areas with a large number of blind customers, businesses often accomodate
them voluntarily. There was an article in the local paper last year about
a pizza joint that got Braille menus made up, added a delivery service,
and has nearly doubled their business (they are within driving range of
the Perkins School for the Blind).

I suspect that enclaves of people with similar needs would likely 
spring up (in fact, probably are, even without a truly free market).


