Date: Tue, 24 Aug 1993 16:38:10 -0400
From: "Perry E. Metzger" <pmetzger@lehman.com>
Subject: PPL Questions 

Lefty says:
> I'm wondering how a couple of situations might be handled in a PPL.
> 
> The first is the outfit from Georgia that's been proposing putting up
> mile-long billboards into low Earth orbit.  In addition to being unsightly
> and annoying, they would seriously screw astronomical observation.  What
> could be done (if anything) about this situation in a PPL?

Well, the real question is one of precedent. Presumably, having your
observation space blocked is similar in a common law sense to having
your view or your sunlight blocked -- it very likely counts as
something actionable, but what tests would be used are difficult to
say. Part of the problem is that PPL is not so much a system as a
metasystem -- it tells you how law is made, but doesn't tell you that
much about how sticky situations actually would get handled. Only
court precedents end up telling you that.

> The second relates to access to businesses for the handicapped.  Currently,
> many businesses are required by law to make themselves accessible to the
> handicapped in various ways, e.g. put in ramps in addition to stairs,
> provide braille menus, etc.  There can't really be sufficient economic
> incentive to do these sort of things: how many blind customers does the
> average restaurant get?  Would the handicapped simply be SOL in a PPL?

I suspect that this would be the case at many facilities, but that
there would be a class of facilities that specialized in serving the
handicapped. This is, IMHO, a good thing -- making every restaurant in
the world work for the handicapped drives up the cost of restaurant
food and hurts the economy. Its better if people pay for that which
they use -- so the cost of handicapped access should be borne by the
handicapped. I realize that this offends some egalitarians, but I
don't see that our goal should be to make the world as "nice" as
possible. This argument, extended, leads to the conclusion that we
should all hire mentally deficient people for jobs that they can't do
because its "nice", and that we should never fire incompetant people
because its "not nice", etc.

Perry

