Date: Tue, 24 Aug 93 09:29:50 PDT
From: brt!robert@uu.psi.com (Robert Blumen)
Subject: EYES: Bates exercises (was Keratotomy)

	Hmmm... the same could be said for any effective method in any field, from
	neurosurgery to termite control.  "Doctors have a vested interest in
	squashing the Salk vaccine, since if it works it could cut off their
	lucrative polio business."  How is it then that effective new methods keep
	cropping up and displacing older techniques in virtually every field?

I do think that it is somewhat true in medicine that when doctors
have a vested interest in suppressing a new technique that they
may be able to do so.  

It is the case in any field that people who are doing things one
way could be out of business if new ways of doing things come along,
and they don't change.  The question is, do the practitioners of
the old ways have the ability to suppress the new ways?   Practitioners
of old techniques might not like to change, but what choice do they
have unless they have some ability to suppress the new ways.

If the 
field in question is reasonably free of regulation and monopolization
(meaning state grants of monopoloy privilege) then the consumers
of the provided service will be free to select among their choice
of older and newer techniques.

Medicine, however, is highly regulated and monopolized.  There are
myriad regulations about who can and cannot practice and what they
can and cannot do.  New techniques are subject to years of regulatory
scrutiny before they can be adopted.  Alternative practitioners
within mainstream medicine or in alternaive fields such as acupuncture
often face legal restrictions and barriers.

All of these factors combine to slow the introduction of new techniques
and to restrict consumer choice which could drive the adoption
of new techniques.

Robert


