Date: Tue, 24 Aug 93 17:41:04 PDT
From: martino@gomez.Jpl.Nasa.Gov (Martin R. Olah)
Subject: TECH: Solar Costs/Bubble Memory

I spent one and a half years growing silicon crystals for Solec International
and I can attest to the high costs involved in producing photovoltaics. 
Eco-Freaks (and others) would flip if they knew the amount of electricity
and nasty chemical processing that went into making such a high priced and
clean source of power. The main advantage of PV's is grid independance, but you
pay for it. As for bringing the price down, new processes will be needed. The 
traditional single-grain crystal grown in a furnace is basically the same 
procedure as used in the semiconductor industry, and I believe lots of money
has already been spent on that process. Solec is switching over to super-
cooling which decreases the time and electricity needed, but there is a limit
to the efficiency that can be gained. The future will surely bring new and 
better ideas, and research funds would help, but it's not a conspiracy or 
short-sightedness that's keeping PV's expensive.
    It just so happens that our furnaces used little plastic bubble memory
keys for changing operating programs. I thought this was pretty handy and
I'd like to here from anyone who knows just why bubble memory isn't more
prevalent.
                                       - O Martin


