> Martin R. Olah says:
> >     It just so happens that our furnaces used little plastic bubble memory
> > keys for changing operating programs. I thought this was pretty handy and
> > I'd like to here from anyone who knows just why bubble memory isn't more
> > prevalent.
> 
> More expensive, slower and lower density than battery backed up CMOS
> SRAM or DRAM. That pretty much sums it up.
> 
> Perry

Well, Bubble Memory wasn't all that bad. After all, were it not for it, I
may not have my current job. ?? Well, I guess the Intel site I work at was
started in hopes of being the first production Bubble Memory fab plant back
in 1984 or something like that (??). Out of the ashes of failure arose a
very successful addition to the Intel US operations ... 8-)

Seriously, though, to expand upon what Perry said, I think that primarily,
just as everyone was about to invest in Bubble, DRAM prices came way down. I
think the original motiviation was to find a more cost-effective memory
solution, back when DRAM was low-density and expensive. CPU clock speeds also
increased quickly - slow bubble memory obviously wasn't the answer. I _have_
heard that a number of technologies today began with the work with bubble
memory, but I don't recall what they are. I believe the research into bubble
memory even aided in the invention of flash memory indirectly. 

